WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.430
The most potent enemy of the Jedi Order is
returning… but how?

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.160
A new rumor has surfaced around the return
of Emperor Palpatine, AKA Darth Sidious , in

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.760
the upcoming Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise
of Skywalker.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.450
It indicates that there won't be any arcane
rituals or Sith trickery involved - rather,

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.400
that the explanation for the Emperor's return
will be an exceedingly simple one.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.820
The Emperor seemed to have met a decisive
end in his final screen appearance, but the

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.439
first trailer for The Rise of Skywalker teased
us with the sound of the villain's distinctive

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.020
laugh.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.860
Since the spot's release, it's become public
that Ian McDiarmid, who portrayed the character

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:43.000
in all of his previous appearances, is involved
with the new film - leading to fan speculation

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.550
that Palpatine may appear in some kind of
vision, or as a Force Ghost, or even return

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.340
as a clone.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.180
"As you know in Star Wars, they're very good
at dealing in unexpected moments the whole

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.700
time, and secrets are a big thing."

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.660
According to some new purported spoilers for
Episode IX making the rounds on the internet,

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.800
the real explanation is quite a bit more mundane.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.720
Instead of coming back as a ghost or spirit,
it's being claimed that the Emperor has actually

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.740
just been physically alive this whole time,
waiting for the right moment to make his presence

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.140
known once again.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.200
Hey, weirder things have happened.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.760
Fans will remember that at the conclusion
of 1983's Return of the Jedi, the last time

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.240
in the series timeline that we saw Palpatine
in the flesh, the Emperor was thwarted in

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.480
his plan to turn Luke Skywalker to the Dark
Side of the Force.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.900
The Emperor pitted Skywalker against his father,
Darth Vader, in a duel to the death, hoping

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.780
to take the young Jedi on as his new apprentice
if he managed to kill the Sith Lord.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:47.140
But at the last moment, Skywalker realized
that he was being manipulated, and refused

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.060
to continue the fight.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.200
Enraged, the Emperor began torturing Luke
with bolts of Force lightning, and he surely

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.880
would have killed the Jedi if not for an abrupt
face turn by Vader.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.350
The former Anakin Skywalker was unexpectedly
moved by Luke calling out to him, and in a

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:07.290
stunning act of self-sacrifice, he seized
the Emperor and threw him to his apparent

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:12.160
death down a seemingly bottomless shaft, mortally
wounding himself in the process.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.880
The redeeming act appeared to finally bring
balance to the Force and restored Vader, in

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.560
the final moments of his life, to his former
self; unfortunately, after revealing his face

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.660
to his son for the first time, Vader succumbed
to his injuries.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:30.060
It was a dramatic and stirring climax to the
original Star Wars trilogy, but it certainly

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:34.700
didn't seem to leave much leeway for the character
of the Emperor to return - outside of the

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.700
prequels, of course.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.760
But now, it seems that Disney, Lucasfilm,
and J.J. Abrams are choosing to bring Palpatine

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.560
back for the conclusion of the Skywalker Saga
in Episode IX.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.919
If these new rumors about how they're doing
it are true, then the filmmakers apparently

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.540
chose to go with the simplest possible explanation.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.069
Further rumors state that those killer-looking,
red Sith Troopers that have been teased at

00:02:56.069 --> 00:03:01.230
conventions and in promo materials are part
of a force assembled by none other than Palpatine

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:01.720
himself.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.540
They will apparently be there to help Palpatine
achieve his goal in the new movie, attempting

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.250
to lure Rey to the Dark Side, in what would
basically amount to a do-over of his failed

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.560
plan to make a Sith apprentice out of Luke.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.080
"Again it's like poetry, sort of.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:15.840
They rhyme."

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.250
The question is, will fans actually like it
if this is how Palpatine comes back?

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.779
The first film of Disney's sequel trilogy,
The Force Awakens, was a crowd-pleasing hit

00:03:25.779 --> 00:03:31.030
that made all of the money when it saw release
in 2015 - but it also drew fire in some quarters

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.320
for essentially being an elaborate retelling
of the original film.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.559
Bringing Palpatine back to basically get up
to his same old tricks seems like it would

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:43.089
invite the same kind of criticism, and to
simply hand-wave the end of Return of the

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:48.780
Jedi with the explanation "Oh, he survived
that" would definitely add more fuel to that

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.280
fire.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.900
But again, weirder things have happened.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.160
An open question is how this new purported
plot leak seems to contradict earlier reports

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.781
that pegged Palpatine as returning in a more
spiritual form, taking possession of a dark

00:04:01.781 --> 00:04:05.620
side acolyte before ultimately finding his
way into Kylo Ren.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.860
Is it possible both versions of the supposed
script leaks are true?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.680
Or are both equally bunk?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.120
"A good question for another time."

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.380
For the time being, we'll just have to wait
to see how it all shakes out until Star Wars

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.600
Episode IX: The Rise of Skywalker hits the
big screen on December 20.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.440
We know one thing, though: if Palpatine does
indeed return to try and entice Rey over to

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:34.090
the Dark Side, he'd better make sure to steer
clear of any bottomless shafts, especially

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.240
if Kylo Ren is anywhere in the vicinity.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.940
Those dark disciples can turn on you in a
hurry.

